1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,053
Some texts you write again and again, and some texts you want to

2
00:00:05,053 --> 00:00:07,195
send to many customers at once.

3
00:00:07,195 --> 00:00:11,421
This video shows the two tools of the messaging workspace for that:

4
00:00:11,421 --> 00:00:17,153
templates, which are canned responses, and broadcasts, which send one message to many people.

5
00:00:17,153 --> 00:00:20,800
It also explains what happens when a customer texts STOP.

6
00:00:25,400 --> 00:00:28,412
Agents answer the same questions all day:

7
00:00:28,412 --> 00:00:32,770
when the shop opens, where it is, what the return policy says.

8
00:00:33,520 --> 00:00:39,101
Typing those answers again is slow, and every agent words them a little differently.

9
00:00:39,101 --> 00:00:43,070
A template gives everyone the same answer, one click away.

10
00:00:43,820 --> 00:00:47,742
And when the shop has news for many customers, such as a sale,

11
00:00:47,742 --> 00:00:50,511
you do not want to send each text by hand.

12
00:00:50,511 --> 00:00:52,530
A broadcast sends it for you.

13
00:00:56,733 --> 00:00:58,880
Open Messaging, Templates.

14
00:00:58,880 --> 00:01:02,906
This is the list of canned responses, and it is empty.

15
00:01:02,906 --> 00:01:04,583
Click Add template.

16
00:01:05,034 --> 00:01:09,065
Give the template a Name that agents will recognize, and write the Body,

17
00:01:09,065 --> 00:01:12,114
which is the text that will be inserted into a reply.

18
00:01:12,569 --> 00:01:13,512
Click Save.

19
00:01:13,512 --> 00:01:18,005
The template joins the list, with the beginning of its text under the name.

20
00:01:18,005 --> 00:01:22,444
Anyone who can use the messaging workspace can insert it, and managing the

21
00:01:22,444 --> 00:01:25,939
list needs the Manage the messaging workspace permission.

22
00:01:30,131 --> 00:01:32,670
In the inbox, open a conversation.

23
00:01:32,670 --> 00:01:38,001
Above the message box, the list Insert a canned response holds your templates.

24
00:01:38,456 --> 00:01:41,787
Pick one, and its text fills the message box.

25
00:01:41,787 --> 00:01:43,420
It is only a draft:

26
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:47,796
you can change it, add the customer's name, and then send it.

27
00:01:51,982 --> 00:01:55,417
Open Messaging, Broadcasts, and click New broadcast.

28
00:01:55,417 --> 00:02:00,332
Each person receives the message as their own one-to-one text, and cannot see

29
00:02:00,332 --> 00:02:01,930
who else received it.

30
00:02:01,930 --> 00:02:05,542
You need the Send group messages permission to do this.

31
00:02:05,992 --> 00:02:09,394
Give the broadcast a Name that only your team sees.

32
00:02:09,394 --> 00:02:13,214
Under Send from, choose the number that sends the message.

33
00:02:13,214 --> 00:02:15,422
The number decides the channel.

34
00:02:15,877 --> 00:02:20,399
Under Recipients, search your contacts and leads by name or by number, and

35
00:02:20,399 --> 00:02:22,150
pick as many as you like.

36
00:02:22,150 --> 00:02:25,767
Only people who have a number on this channel are offered.

37
00:02:30,573 --> 00:02:35,677
Under Additional addresses, add numbers that are not in your contacts, one per

38
00:02:35,677 --> 00:02:37,742
line or separated by commas.

39
00:02:37,742 --> 00:02:41,873
Write them in international format, starting with a plus sign.

40
00:02:42,327 --> 00:02:45,347
Write the Message, and click Queue broadcast.

41
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:53,529
The list shows the broadcast with its channel, the number it is sent

42
00:02:53,529 --> 00:02:59,240
from, its status, how many recipients it has, and how many messages were sent and failed.

43
00:02:59,240 --> 00:03:04,230
It starts as Queued, because the system sends the messages in the background.

44
00:03:04,230 --> 00:03:08,679
When every recipient has been handled, the status becomes Completed.

45
00:03:08,679 --> 00:03:13,730
Failed counts the messages the provider refused, and the people who opted out.

46
00:03:13,730 --> 00:03:18,720
A queued broadcast cannot be cancelled, so read it twice before you queue it.

47
00:03:22,920 --> 00:03:26,011
Every recipient gets a private conversation.

48
00:03:26,011 --> 00:03:31,080
When a customer replies, the reply arrives in the inbox like any other text.

49
00:03:31,830 --> 00:03:36,211
If the provider does not accept a message, it is tried again after

50
00:03:36,211 --> 00:03:40,470
one, five, fifteen and sixty minutes before it is marked failed.

51
00:03:41,220 --> 00:03:46,436
A broadcast does not wait for quiet hours, and it does not check your do-not-call lists.

52
00:03:46,436 --> 00:03:49,600
Send it only to people who agreed to hear from you.

53
00:03:53,800 --> 00:03:59,542
A customer who texts STOP, or one of the words that mean the same, is unsubscribed.

54
00:03:59,542 --> 00:04:04,574
The conversation is closed, the contact is marked Do not S.M.S., and the

55
00:04:04,574 --> 00:04:07,220
customer receives one confirmation.

56
00:04:07,970 --> 00:04:11,102
A customer who texts START is subscribed again.

57
00:04:11,102 --> 00:04:15,358
This works only for people who opted out, so an ordinary yes stays

58
00:04:15,358 --> 00:04:17,190
part of the conversation.

59
00:04:17,940 --> 00:04:23,362
A customer who texts HELP receives the help reply, which tells them how to unsubscribe.

60
00:04:23,362 --> 00:04:27,560
None of these keywords triggers the auto-reply of the entry point.

61
00:04:28,310 --> 00:04:31,636
A broadcast does not reach people who opted out.

62
00:04:31,636 --> 00:04:35,454
Their message is marked failed at once, with the reason:

63
00:04:35,454 --> 00:04:37,980
The contact has opted out of S.M.S.

64
00:04:39,576 --> 00:04:41,971
Here is a customer who texted STOP.

65
00:04:41,971 --> 00:04:46,468
The thread shows the word, and says that the conversation is closed and

66
00:04:46,468 --> 00:04:48,045
cannot be replied to.

67
00:04:48,045 --> 00:04:52,076
The customer will not receive broadcasts until they text START.

68
00:04:56,276 --> 00:05:01,224
A bike shop saves its opening hours, its address and its return policy

69
00:05:01,224 --> 00:05:04,936
as templates, so every agent gives the same answer.

70
00:05:05,686 --> 00:05:10,221
A clinic broadcasts a closing notice to its patients the day before a

71
00:05:10,221 --> 00:05:13,366
holiday, and answers the replies in the inbox.

72
00:05:14,116 --> 00:05:18,220
A shop announces a weekend sale to the customers who asked for offers,

73
00:05:18,220 --> 00:05:20,596
and leaves out everyone who opted out.

74
00:05:22,596 --> 00:05:27,240
Save your common answers as templates, and use a broadcast when many customers

75
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:28,788
need the same message.

76
00:05:28,788 --> 00:05:30,226
Thanks for watching.

